1. Cases of Personal Pronouns

A pronoun can have different forms depending on its function.

                        | Person        | Subjective/Nominative | Objective | Possessive Adjective | Possessive Pronoun |
                        | ------------- | --------------------- | --------- | -------------------- | ------------------ |
                        | 1st singular  | **I**                 | **me**    | **my**               | **mine**           |
                        | 1st plural    | **we**                | **us**    | **our**              | **ours**           |
                        | 2nd           | **you**               | **you**   | **your**             | **yours**          |
                        | 3rd masculine | **he**                | **him**   | **his**              | **his**            |
                        | 3rd feminine  | **she**               | **her**   | **her**              | **hers**           |
                        | 3rd neuter    | **it**                | **it**    | **its**              | —                  |
                        | 3rd plural    | **they**              | **them**  | **their**            | **theirs**         |


Example

I like my car.
This car is mine.

I → subject
my → possessive adjective
mine → possessive pronoun

2. Pronouns joined by AND / OR

When two or more pronouns are joined by and/or, they should normally be in the same case.

Subjective case

You and I will do the work. ✅

Both are subjects:

You → subject
I → subject

❌ You and me will do the work.

Objective case

The teacher called you and me. ✅

Both are objects:

you → object
me → object

❌ The teacher called you and I.

Another example

The gift is for Ram and me. ✅

Not:

❌ The gift is for Ram and I.

Because for is a preposition and requires the objective form.

3. After “LET” → Objective Case

After let, use an objective pronoun.

Let him go. ✅
Let her speak. ✅
Let us attend the meeting. ✅

Not:

❌ Let he go.
❌ Let she speak.
❌ Let we attend.

Why?

Let + object + V1

Let him + go.

So:

LET → objective case

4. After “It is / It was” → Subjective Case

According to the traditional exam rule, use the subjective form after:

It is...
It was...

Examples:

It was he who helped me. ✅

It is I who am responsible. ✅

Not, according to this traditional rule:

❌ It was him who helped me.
❌ It is me who is responsible.

Why?

The pronoun is considered the subject complement, so the subjective case is preferred in formal/traditional grammar.


5. “If I were...” → Subjective Case

After were, in the traditional construction:

If I were he, I would help her.

Not:

❌ If I were him...

Why?

Because he is functioning as a subject complement.

Remember:

If I were he... ✅
If I were she... ✅

This is the traditional competitive-exam rule.


6. “Suppose” / “Supposed” type construction

The notes also give examples involving suppose:

I supposed Ram to be him.

Here him is in the objective case because it follows the object/complement construction.

The important point is to identify the grammatical function, rather than simply choosing a pronoun by position.


7. Pronoun after “THAN”

This is the part you asked about earlier.

After than, both subjective and objective forms can be possible, depending on the meaning.

Example 1

I know better than he.

This can mean:

I know better than he does.

So he is the subject of the implied does.

Example 2

I know better than him.

This can mean:

I know better than I know him.

So him is the object.

Therefore:

I know better than he/him.

The correct choice depends on the intended meaning.

8. Your example: “I like chocolates more than he/him.”

This is exactly why Rule 34 is important.

Meaning 1

I like chocolates more than he does.

→ he ✅

Full sentence:

I like chocolates more than he likes chocolates.

Here he = subject.

Meaning 2

I like chocolates more than I like him.

→ him ✅

Here him = object.

So both can be grammatically correct, but they give different meanings.


        
